# /etc/cron.d/fpl-buddy
#
# Four jobs on different clocks - see deploy/README.md for the reasoning.
#   deadline-watch  : hourly, must be timing-accurate (FPL deadlines are irregular)
#   daily-refresh   : 03:00, deliberately late (BPS/data_checked lags late kickoffs)
#   gameweek-report : 03:15, after the refresh, before IndexNow reads the sitemap
#   indexnow        : 03:30, after the refresh has rewritten the player pages
#
# CHECK THE CONTAINER NAME FIRST. `docker exec` against a name that doesn't
# exist fails silently from cron - the job simply never runs, and the only
# symptom is that processed_deadline and ai_team_snapshot stay empty forever,
# which looks exactly like "the feature isn't working". Confirm with:
#
#   docker ps --format '{{.Names}}'
#
# The name below is `fpl-buddy` because docker-compose.yml now pins
# `container_name`. Without that pin Compose derives the name from the
# directory the stack lives in - which is why this used to read
# `apps-fpl-companion-1` rather than anything chosen on purpose. If you are
# running a compose file WITHOUT the pin, expect `<dir>-fpl-buddy-1` instead
# and correct every line below.
#
# Install with:
#   cp deploy/fpl-buddy.cron /etc/cron.d/fpl-buddy && chmod 644 /etc/cron.d/fpl-buddy
#
# If you are migrating from the old name, delete the old file - two cron files
# both running these jobs is not harmless. `deadline-watch` is idempotent, but
# `daily-refresh` would run the whole pipeline twice at 03:00 on a 4-core box:
#   rm -f /etc/cron.d/fpl-companion
#
# No secrets are passed here on purpose. `docker exec` inherits the container's
# own environment, and FPL_REFRESH_TOKEN and FPL_INDEXNOW_KEY are already set
# there by compose, so reading them from a file on the host would just be a
# second copy to keep in sync. The two jobs.py jobs call /api/refresh inside the
# container over 127.0.0.1; indexnow.py reads its key the same inherited way.

SHELL=/bin/sh
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin

# TZ matters for the 03:00 slot only; the deadline watcher reads UTC timestamps
# from the API and doesn't care what the host clock is set to.
CRON_TZ=Europe/London

# The container name is written out in full on every line rather than held in a
# variable. Variable expansion in cron.d depends on the job shell rather than on
# cron itself, and the failure mode if it doesn't expand is the silent one this
# file's header warns about. Literals that are obviously wrong beat one
# indirection that might be.

# Hourly, at 5 past - a few minutes clear of the top of the hour so it isn't
# competing with every other cron on the box.
5 * * * * root docker exec fpl-buddy python jobs.py deadline-watch >> /var/log/fpl-buddy-watch.log 2>&1

# Daily heavy refresh + backfill of actual scores onto frozen snapshots.
0 3 * * * root docker exec fpl-buddy python jobs.py daily-refresh >> /var/log/fpl-buddy-daily.log 2>&1

# Rebuild the gameweek page. After the refresh, so it reports today's form,
# prices and injury news rather than yesterday's.
#
# Safe to run every night for the whole season: once a gameweek's deadline
# passes, the hourly watcher freezes that edition and this job skips it. It can
# never rewrite a published archive page - which is the only thing that would
# make the /gameweek archive dishonest.
#
# The 03:15 slot is load-bearing in one direction: it must come BEFORE the
# IndexNow ping at 03:30, because that script reads the live sitemap and a new
# gameweek page that doesn't exist yet won't be submitted until tomorrow.
15 3 * * * root docker exec fpl-buddy python jobs.py gameweek-report >> /var/log/fpl-buddy-report.log 2>&1

# Tell Bing (and the other IndexNow engines) what changed overnight. Half an
# hour after the refresh starts, so it reports the day's data rather than
# yesterday's - the refresh rewrites the per-player pages the sitemap lists.
#
# Not passed a key: FPL_INDEXNOW_KEY is already in the container's environment
# and docker exec inherits it, same as the refresh token above. If the variable
# is unset the app serves no key file and the script exits non-zero with a
# clear message rather than silently doing nothing.
30 3 * * * root docker exec fpl-buddy python indexnow.py >> /var/log/fpl-buddy-indexnow.log 2>&1

# Pull Search Console, Bing and PageSpeed into state/seo/<date>.json.
#
# 04:00 rather than alongside the others: the PageSpeed calls take 10-30
# seconds each and this is the one job nothing else waits on, so it gets the
# quiet slot after the pipeline has finished competing for four cores.
#
# Daily even though Search Console only updates once a day and lags 2-3 days
# behind. The window it asks for ends three days back, so consecutive files
# overlap heavily - that redundancy is the point. It means a few nights of
# failed runs cost nothing, because the next successful one still carries the
# whole 28-day window.
0 4 * * * root docker exec fpl-buddy python seo_report.py >> /var/log/fpl-buddy-seo.log 2>&1
